      title: "Почему падают империи",
      text: "Every schoolchild knows the story. Rome grew fat on plunder, its citizens grew soft, and the barbarian hordes simply walked through the gates. It is a satisfying tale, with a clear moral, and it is almost certainly wrong. Historians who have spent their careers in the archives of far-flung provinces tell a messier, and far more interesting, story.\n\nConsider the problem of distance. At its height, Roman dominion stretched from the misty hinterland of northern Britain to the deserts of Mesopotamia. A message from a frontier outpost on the Danube might have taken three weeks to reach the capital, and the emperor's reply another three to come back. By the time orders arrived, the situation they addressed must often have changed beyond recognition. Governors cannot have waited patiently for instructions; they will have improvised, and in improvising they gradually built their own power bases. Many a provincial command became, in effect, a private fiefdom.\n\nThen there is money. Garrisons had to be fed, paid and equipped, and the levies needed to sustain them fell hardest on the very peasants whose loyalty the empire depended on. Scholars use the term imperial overstretch for the point at which the cost of holding territory exceeds what that territory yields. Rome may well have reached that point by the third century, when a succession of short reigns, many of them seized by usurpers raised by their own legions, meant that emperors spent more energy fighting one another than defending the borders.\n\nThe Persian Achaemenids had faced a similar dilemma centuries earlier. Their solution was to divide the realm into satrapies, rule through local elites and exact tribute rather than demand cultural conformity. For two hundred years this light touch worked remarkably well. It could also be argued that it planted the seeds of fragmentation, since a satrap who controlled his own army and treasury needed the King of Kings only as long as the King of Kings was strong.\n\nNone of this means that empires are doomed by some iron law. The Byzantine state, Rome's eastern half, survived for a thousand years after the West collapsed, partly by turning neighbours into client states and buffer states rather than trying to subjugate them outright. When it did launch punitive expeditions, it usually withdrew afterwards instead of attempting annexation.\n\nWhat, then, of decadence? The word tells us more about the moralists who used it than about the societies they described. Roman writers complained about luxury and lost virtue at the very moment the empire was expanding fastest. They can't have been describing a cause of decline, because the decline had not yet begun. The real lesson is less comforting: empires rarely fall because their people become weak. They fall because the machinery that holds them together becomes too expensive, too slow, and too easy for ambitious men to capture.",
