      text: "It is the morning of 23 February 1917, and in the industrial suburbs of Petrograd the bread queues are longer than ever. It is International Women's Day, and the women who work in the textile mills of the Vyborg district have had enough. They walk out, march to neighbouring factories and call on the men to join them. Nobody, least of all the revolutionary parties, has planned this. Lenin is in exile in Zurich, and he has recently told a meeting of young socialists that his generation may not live to see the revolution.\n\nWithin two days, the strike has become general. Trams stop running, newspapers fail to appear, and crowds flood the city centre carrying red banners and home-made placards demanding bread and an end to the war. The authorities respond as they always have. Nicholas II, who is at army headquarters hundreds of miles away and has little grasp of the scale of the unrest, sends a curt telegram ordering the disorders to be stopped by the following day. On the 26th, troops open fire on demonstrators. Dozens are killed. For a few hours, it looks as though the clampdown has worked.\n\nThen something extraordinary happens. That night, the soldiers of the Volynsky Regiment, many of them peasant conscripts barely out of training, argue among themselves in their barracks. They have been ordered to shoot people who look exactly like their own mothers and sisters. In the morning, they shoot their commanding officer instead and march out to join the crowds. Mutiny spreads from regiment to regiment like fire through dry grass. By evening, the arsenal has been seized, the prisons have been thrown open, and the city belongs to the insurgents.\n\nPower now passes, uneasily, to two bodies at once. Liberal politicians from the Duma form what will become the Provisional Government, while in another wing of the same building workers and soldiers elect a Soviet. Each needs the other; neither trusts the other. On 2 March, in a railway carriage near Pskov, the tsar is persuaded by his own generals to abdicate. Three centuries of Romanov rule end not with a storming of the palace but with a signature.\n\nIt is worth pausing on that detail. The image of revolution most of us carry, the image of barricades, firebrands and a disciplined vanguard leading the masses, comes largely from October, and from the Bolsheviks' own later mythmaking. February was different. It was leaderless, improvised and astonishingly fast. The ancien régime did not so much fall as dissolve, because the men whose job was to defend it decided, almost overnight, that they would not.\n\nHistorians still argue about whether the collapse was inevitable. What is beyond dispute is that the tumult of those eight days opened a door, and that within a year the people who walked through it would sweep away the liberals, the moderates of the Soviet and much else besides.",
