      minWords: 260, use: ["were it not for", "but for", "the historical record", "contingency"],
      model: "The saying that history is written by the victors contains an uncomfortable truth, but it is also, in my view, a misleading simplification.\n\nIt is certainly true that power shapes the historical record. Empires commissioned chronicles, kings employed court historians, and the defeated rarely had the chance to tell their side of the story. Our image of Carthage, for example, comes almost entirely from Roman writers, since the Romans destroyed the city in 146 BC. Were it not for archaeological finds and a few scattered Greek accounts, we would know Carthage only as the villain of someone else's epic.\n\nHowever, the victors do not keep control of the narrative for ever. Over time, historians return to forgotten sources, question official versions and give a voice to those who were silenced. The American Civil War is a striking case. For decades after 1865, many popular accounts in the South portrayed the Confederacy as a noble lost cause, which shows that the losers can sometimes write history very effectively indeed.\n\nMoreover, the phrase ignores contingency. Victory itself was often a matter of chance. Had the weather been different on a particular day, or had a single commander made a different decision, the roles of winner and loser might have been reversed, and the textbooks along with them. But for this awareness, we would be tempted to see the winners as somehow destined to win.\n\nIn conclusion, the victors usually write the first draft of history, but not the last. The real task of the historian is to keep revising that draft, listening carefully for the voices it leaves out."
