      text: "On a June afternoon in 2020, protesters in Bristol pulled down a bronze statue of Edward Colston, a seventeenth-century merchant whose fortune was built in large part on the transatlantic slave trade, and rolled it into the harbour. Within days, the statue had been fished out of the water by the city council. It now lies, graffiti and all, in a local museum, accompanied by an exhibition explaining both Colston's life and the events of that afternoon. The object has, in other words, acquired a second history, and arguably a more honest one than the first.\n\nThe Colston episode crystallised a debate that had been simmering for years in countries as different as South Africa, the United States and Belgium. Public statues are not neutral vestiges of the past. Most were erected long after the events they commemorate, by people who wished to tell a particular story about who deserved to be honoured. A city that put a slave trader on a pedestal in 1895 was making a claim about its own values, and those who topple the statue today are, whether they say so or not, making a counterclaim. What critics call the erasure of history might more accurately be described as a dispute about which history is to be memorialised in the town square.\n\nMuseums face a parallel question, in which the issue is not what to display but to whom it belongs. The so-called Benin Bronzes, thousands of plaques and sculptures looted by British troops during a punitive expedition against the Kingdom of Benin in 1897, ended up scattered among collections in London, Berlin, Vienna and elsewhere. For decades, institutions argued that they were the best custodians of such objects, which, having been preserved by skilled conservators, could be seen by millions of visitors. That argument has steadily lost ground. In 2022 Germany began formally returning its holdings to Nigeria, and several British institutions followed. Supporters see repatriation as overdue restitution; sceptics worry about precedent, asking whether every great museum will eventually be emptied.\n\nNeither position is as simple as its loudest advocates suggest. Returning an object cannot undo a historical wrong, and no serious campaigner claims it can. Nor does keeping a statue in place amount to endorsing everything its subject did. What both debates reveal is that heritage is never simply something we receive. It is something we choose, interpret and argue over, generation after generation.\n\nPerhaps that is the most useful lesson. A society that reckons with its past honestly will not agree on everything, but it will at least acknowledge that monuments and museum collections are made by people, for reasons, and that those reasons can be questioned. The past cannot be changed. How we live with it, and what we bequeath to posterity, can.",
