    text: "Few images from antiquity are as haunting as that of the Library of Alexandria in flames, the accumulated wisdom of the ancient world curling into ash in a single catastrophic night. The story has been told by poets, novelists and television presenters, and its moral seems obvious: civilisation is fragile, and ignorance is always waiting at the gates. The trouble is that the single night almost certainly never happened.\n\nWhat we know about the library is frustratingly little. It was founded in the third century BC under the Ptolemies, the Greek-speaking dynasty that ruled Egypt after the death of Alexander the Great, and it was undoubtedly ambitious. According to the physician Galen, ships docking at Alexandria were searched for books, which were then copied; the library kept the originals and returned the copies to their owners. Estimates of the collection range from forty thousand scrolls to seven hundred thousand, a spread so wide that it tells us less about the library than about the enthusiasm of those doing the estimating.\n\nThe question of its destruction is even murkier. Several culprits have been proposed over the centuries. Julius Caesar, besieged in the city in 48 BC, set fire to ships in the harbour, and the flames may well have spread to warehouses near the docks where books were stored. Yet the geographer Strabo, who worked in Alexandria a couple of decades later, describes its scholarly community without mentioning any disaster. Had the library been wiped out by Caesar, it seems unlikely that Strabo would have passed over it in silence. In the 270s AD, the emperor Aurelian's war against the breakaway Palmyrene empire devastated the palace quarter in which the library seems to have stood. In 391, a Christian crowd encouraged by the bishop Theophilus destroyed the Serapeum, a temple which housed a smaller daughter library. And the story that the Arab general Amr ibn al-As burned the remaining books on the caliph's orders in 642 first appears in sources written some six hundred years after the event, which is why most historians regard it as apocryphal.\n\nThe most likely explanation is the least dramatic. Royal patronage dwindled; scholars were expelled during political purges in the second century BC and moved elsewhere; papyrus rotted in the humid air of the Nile delta unless it was recopied, and recopying required both money and will. The library, in other words, did not die so much as fade, its decline spread across centuries and recorded by nobody in particular.\n\nWhy, then, does the myth of the great fire persist? Partly because a single villain makes a better story than a slow loss of funding. But the myth also serves whoever tells it. Enlightenment writers blamed Christian fanatics; others blamed Muslim conquerors; modern commentators have used the library as a warning about cuts to public education. Each version reveals the anxieties of its own age at least as clearly as it illuminates the ancient past. That, perhaps, is the real lesson of Alexandria: the stories we tell about lost knowledge are rarely about the past alone.",
