    model: "The Iron Crown, a new drama about the Empress Matilda's long and bitter war for the English throne in the twelfth century, is lavish, gripping and, in places, wildly unreliable. Whether that matters depends on what you expect from a historical film.\n\nThe production values are superb. Filmed in real castles and lit almost entirely by candle and firelight, it captures how cold, dark and cramped medieval life must have been, even for royalty. The battle scenes are chaotic rather than choreographed, which is probably far closer to the truth than the neat formations of most epics.\n\nThe script is another matter. The film's most memorable sequence shows Matilda escaping from a besieged castle across a frozen river, dressed in white to hide against the snow. The episode does appear in medieval chronicles, but some historians suspect it is at least partly apocryphal, and the film presents it without the slightest hint of doubt. More troubling is an invented romance with a young knight, which turns a shrewd political operator into the heroine of a conventional melodrama. Were it not for a brief caption at the end, viewers might leave believing every word.\n\nWhat the film does well is to show the extent to which the conflict was driven by ordinary ambition rather than grand principle. Barons switch sides whenever it suits them, towns are burned for no strategic reason, and the peasants who pay for everything barely register in the eyes of their rulers. In these scenes, the director seems genuinely interested in the period rather than in using it as a costume for a modern story.\n\nOverall, The Iron Crown is worth seeing, provided you treat it as an imaginative interpretation rather than a documentary. Had the writers trusted their audience a little more, and used their sources a little less selectively, it could have been a masterpiece. As it stands, it is a handsome, enjoyable and frustrating film."
